db/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533952 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533952 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186533224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533952 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f19bbfd2-a4f0-5fac-92d7-e5b144aac77f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533966 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533268 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533966 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533966 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533966 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533966 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533966 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533966 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186533389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533966 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fc4487a4-144f-5b03-86d5-0ad215d73311" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533977 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533977 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533977 #edb/ref 1069 "q-333626" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533977 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533977 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has only one eigenvalue. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.\n\n$\\,$ The equilibrium point (origin) is a \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nnode." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533977 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "130e77e3-e68d-5bfc-a672-f433d0d78798" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533977 #edb/ref 1074 "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is $\\lambda =4$ and it's positive, we have a $\\text{unstable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is not diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector. So, we have a $\\text{degenerate}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f6/f67469d62d6a0eb61397927924e55868437ad09f405c3b2a9d73567af00c4620.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534013 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534013 #edb/ref 1069 "q-333296" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534013 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534013 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-6x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534013 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6296be80-55c8-5a2e-8b17-a0c84de2ed61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534013 #edb/ref 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-6$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-6t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-6t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={e}^{-6t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNotice that for each vector $[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n${e}^{-6t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince ${e}^{-6t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ \n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/ff/ff337e0120343c7c956afa5e49ea3b482fbd79fe363783a018842d1bfeb340d6.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534071 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534071 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533802 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534071 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534071 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534071 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534071 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534071 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534071 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534071 #edb/ref 1069 "q-333601" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534071 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534071 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector $v=[1,\\text{ }-1]^{T}$ corresponds to the repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of the system's matrix $A.$ The vector $w=[0,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{{field-1}}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}{{field-2}}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ -t+1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-2t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/45/4522e4e6ed382e821aaa47d40a191135161f95eef276b6e8ba3a8514e6bfdfd8.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ -t+1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x={{field-6}} \\\\ y=(-t+1){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | {{field-7}} | $0$ | $0.18$ |\n| $y$ | {{field-8}} | $1$ | $0.18$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/db/dbf5a13ecd5e81c399204fd29d235e410fcb62274555cf3e5b32a383c1078d24.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/a6/a680b8c9319290df3beb20d7db964f385b368b01ead33961e2c10d22b19db4eb.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534071 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d04cdee5-5005-58d2-afad-7aaebd536510" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534071 #edb/ref 1074 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ -t+1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-2t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/45/4522e4e6ed382e821aaa47d40a191135161f95eef276b6e8ba3a8514e6bfdfd8.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ -t+1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t{e}^{-2t} \\\\ y=(-t+1){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-1.36$ | $0$ | $0.18$ |\n| $y$ | $4.08$ | $1$ | $0.18$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/db/dbf5a13ecd5e81c399204fd29d235e410fcb62274555cf3e5b32a383c1078d24.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/13/13a57f14ea65118473c24c6eb1dbf50a0461060201ced051bb2eb6776f5daf64.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
